== 13. Private Prayer in Times of Great Straits and Trials

Consider that in times of great straits and trials, in times of great afflictions and persecutions, private prayer is the Christian’s food and drink. It is his chief city of refuge; it is his shelter and hiding place in a stormy day. When the saints have been driven by violent persecutions into holes, and caves, and dens, and deserts, and howling wildernesses, private prayer has been their food and drink, and under Christ their only refuge. When Esau came out with hostile intentions against Jacob, secret prayer was Jacob’s refuge. “When the messengers returned to Jacob, they said, ‘We went to your brother Esau, and now he is coming to meet you—he and four hundred men with him,’” all cutthroats. “In great fear and distress, Jacob divided his people into two camps, as well as the flocks and herds and camels. He thought, ‘If Esau comes and attacks one camp, then the other camp can escape’” (Gen 32:6–8). When all is at stake, it is Christian prudence to save what we can, though we cannot save what we would. Then Jacob said, “O God of my father Abraham, God of my father Isaac, the #smallcaps[Lord] who told me, ‘Go back to your country and to your kindred, and I will make you prosper’” (v. 9). Promises must be prayed over in private. God loves to be sued on His own bond when He and His people are alone. “Please deliver me from the hand of my brother Esau, for I am afraid that he may come and attack me, and the mother with the children” (v. 11)—or “upon the children,” meaning he will put all to death. Some take the words to be a metaphor drawn from fowlers, who kill and carry off the young and the mother birds together, contrary to that old law (Deut 22:6). Others say it is a phrase that most vividly represents the tenderness of a mother who, seeing her children in distress, does not spare her own body or life, but risks them for her children’s preservation by putting herself between, even to be massacred together with them and upon them (Hos 10:14). When Jacob and all that was near and dear to him were in imminent danger of being cut off by Esau and the men of blood who were with him, he turns to private prayer as his only city of refuge against the rage and malice of the mighty.

And so, when Jeremiah was in a solitary and loathsome dungeon, private prayer was his food and drink; it was his only city of refuge. “While Jeremiah was still confined in the courtyard of the guard, the word of the #smallcaps[Lord] came to him a second time: Thus says the #smallcaps[Lord] who made the earth, the #smallcaps[Lord] who formed it and established it, the #smallcaps[Lord] is His name: Call to Me, and I will answer and show you great and unsearchable,” or hidden, “things you do not know” (Jer 33:1–3). When Jeremiah was in a lonesome, loathsome prison, God encourages him by private prayer to seek further disclosures and revelations of the choice and singular favors that He purposed to confer on His people in future times.

“So the #smallcaps[Lord] brought against them the military commanders of the king of Assyria, who captured Manasseh, put a hook in his nose, bound him with bronze shackles,” or chains, “and took him to Babylon. And in his distress, Manasseh sought the favor of the #smallcaps[Lord] his God and earnestly humbled himself before the God of his fathers. And when he prayed to Him, the #smallcaps[Lord] received his plea and heard his petition; so He brought him back to Jerusalem and to his kingdom. Then Manasseh knew that the #smallcaps[Lord] is God” (2 Chr 33:11–13). When Manasseh was in shackles in his enemy’s country, when he was stripped of all his princely glory and led captive into Babylon, he turns to private prayer as his only city of refuge; and by this means he prevails with God for his restoration to his crown and kingdom.

Private prayer is a city of refuge that no power or plotting, no craft or cruelty, no violence or force, is ever able to capture. Though the joint prayers of the people of God together were often obstructed and hindered in the times of the ten persecutions, the persecutors were never able to obstruct or hinder secret prayer, private prayer. When men and devils have done their worst, every Christian will be able to keep up his private trade with heaven. Private prayer will shelter a Christian against all the national, domestic, and personal storms and tempests that may threaten him. When a man is lying on a sickbed alone, or when a man is in prison alone, or when a man is left with Job on the dunghill alone, or when a man is banished with John for the testimony of Jesus to this or that island alone, oh, then private prayer will be his food and drink, his shelter, his hiding place, his heaven. When all other trades fail, this trade of private prayer will hold good.
